What I learned
- You chose Option B: a materialized patient_index container (lightweight documents) kept in sync with patient document changes.
- You want a cost-effective, performant infinite-scroll / paginated patient list implementation that minimizes read RUs and network transfer.
- You plan to store X12 payloads externally (already decided) so index documents remain small.

What I'll deliver here
1. The recommended patient_index document schema.
2. A robust, production-ready Change Feed worker example (C# Azure Function) that keeps the index up-to-date (idempotent upserts).
3. Example Cosmos DB query code to page (infinite scroll) with continuation tokens and PartitionKey usage.
4. RU estimates, operational notes (backfill, deletes, monitoring), and trade-offs.

1) patient_index schema (lightweight)
- Keep each index doc very small (few hundred bytes).
- Fields chosen to support sorting, searching, and list display without loading full patient documents.

Example document:
```json
{
  "id": "pat_a1b2c3d4",          // same as patient id
  "type": "patient_index",
  "tenantId": "tenant_xyz",
  "practiceId": "practice_123",
  "firstName": "Sarah",
  "lastName": "Johnson",
  "dateOfBirth": "1985-03-15T00:00:00Z",
  "mainContact": "+1-555-0123",
  "lastVisitDate": "2024-12-22T09:15:00Z",
  "lastEncounterId": "enc_m3n4o5p6",
  "hasActiveCoverage": true,
  "flags": ["needsConsent"],    // small set of UI flags for filtering
  "lastUpdatedUtc": "2024-12-22T09:45:00Z"
}
```

Fields to include (minimally):
- id (patientId)
- practiceId (partition key)
- lastName, firstName, dateOfBirth (search/sort)
- lastVisitDate (sort/filter)
- mainContact (display)
- lastEncounterId (optional quick link)
- hasActiveCoverage, flags (optional small filters)
- lastUpdatedUtc (ordering / cache invalidation)

2) Change Feed worker (C# Azure Function) — recommended async model
- Use Cosmos DB Change Feed to capture patient container changes and upsert the corresponding patient_index document.
- Use Azure Functions Cosmos DB Trigger (or the ChangeFeedProcessor library). The following is an Azure Function example (stable, straightforward), idempotent, and resilient.

Sample Azure Function (C# .NET 6+):
```csharp
// NuGet: Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.CosmosDB, Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos

using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos;
using Newtonsoft.Json.Linq;

public static class PatientIndexChangeFeed
{
    // Configure these in app settings / Key Vault
    private static readonly string CosmosConnectionString = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("CosmosDBConnection");
    private static readonly string DbName = "bfdb";
    private static readonly string IndexContainerName = "patient_index";

    [FunctionName("PatientIndexChangeFeed")]
    public static async Task Run(
        [CosmosDBTrigger(
            databaseName: "bfdb",
            containerName: "patients",
            Connection = "CosmosDBConnection",
            LeaseContainerName = "leases",
            CreateLeaseContainerIfNotExists = true)] IReadOnlyList<JObject> input,
        ILogger log)
    {
        if (input == null || input.Count == 0) return;

        var client = new CosmosClient(CosmosConnectionString);
        var indexContainer = client.GetContainer(DbName, IndexContainerName);

        foreach (var doc in input)
        {
            try
            {
                // If delete events are supported by your trigger setup, handle them.
                // The trigger gives full documents for creates/updates; deletions require soft-delete flag or separate approach.
                var id = doc.Value<string>("id");
                var practiceId = doc.Value<string>("practiceId");
                if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(id) || string.IsNullOrEmpty(practiceId))
                {
                    log.LogWarning("Skipped change without id/practiceId");
                    continue;
                }

                // Build index doc from patient doc. Select only small set of fields.
                var indexDoc = new
                {
                    id = id,
                    type = "patient_index",
                    tenantId = doc.Value<string>("tenantId"),
                    practiceId = practiceId,
                    firstName = doc.Value<string>("firstName"),
                    lastName = doc.Value<string>("lastName"),
                    dateOfBirth = doc.Value<DateTime?>("dateOfBirth"),
                    mainContact = doc.Value<string>("phone") ?? doc.Value<string>("email"),
                    lastVisitDate = ComputeLastVisitDate(doc), // function below
                    lastEncounterId = GetLastEncounterId(doc),
                    hasActiveCoverage = HasActiveCoverage(doc),
                    lastUpdatedUtc = doc.Value<DateTime?>("updatedAtUtc") ?? DateTime.UtcNow
                };

                var requestOptions = new ItemRequestOptions { };
                // PartitionKey is practiceId
                var pk = new PartitionKey(practiceId);

                // Upsert is idempotent - safe to re-run
                await indexContainer.UpsertItemAsync(indexDoc, pk, requestOptions);
            }
            catch (CosmosException ce)
            {
                log.LogError(ce, "Cosmos error upserting index doc");
                // Optionally re-throw for function retry or log metrics and continue
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                log.LogError(ex, "Error processing change feed doc");
            }
        }
    }

    // Helper examples:
    private static DateTime? ComputeLastVisitDate(JObject patient)
    {
        var encounters = patient["encounters"] as JArray;
        if (encounters == null || encounters.Count == 0) return null;

        DateTime? max = null;
        foreach (var e in encounters)
        {
            if (DateTime.TryParse(e.Value<string>("visitDate"), out var dt))
            {
                if (max == null || dt > max) max = dt;
            }
        }
        return max;
    }

    private static string? GetLastEncounterId(JObject patient)
    {
        var encounters = patient["encounters"] as JArray;
        if (encounters == null || encounters.Count == 0) return null;
        // assumes encounters sorted or pick max by visitDate
        string? lastId = null;
        DateTime? max = null;
        foreach (var e in encounters)
        {
            if (DateTime.TryParse(e.Value<string>("visitDate"), out var dt))
            {
                if (max == null || dt > max)
                {
                    max = dt;
                    lastId = e.Value<string>("id");
                }
            }
        }
        return lastId;
    }

    private static bool HasActiveCoverage(JObject patient)
    {
        var ce = patient["coverageEnrollments"] as JArray;
        if (ce == null) return false;
        foreach (var c in ce)
        {
            if (c.Value<bool?>("isActive") == true) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

Notes:
- The function upserts to patient_index container with PartitionKey = practiceId.
- Use Upsert to be idempotent.
- Prefer asynchronous Change Feed processing (decouples list reads from writes).
- For low-latency requirements you could synchronously upsert index in the API path, but that increases write latency and risk; async via Change Feed is recommended.

3) Initial backfill strategy
- If patient_index does not exist yet, perform an initial backfill:
  - Bulk read patient container by practice (partitioned reads) using paging and create index docs (bulk upserts).
  - Use Cosmos Bulk support (CosmosClient.CreateItemAsync with concurrency) or Azure Data Factory for large migrations.
  - After backfill, enable Change Feed processor so ongoing changes are processed.

4) Query code: paginated list with continuation token (C# Cosmos SDK)
- Query the patient_index container (small docs) partitioned by practiceId.
- Use MaxItemCount/page size and pass continuation token to fetch next page.

Example:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class PatientListDto
{
    public string id { get; set; } = default!;
    public string firstName { get; set; } = default!;
    public string lastName { get; set; } = default!;
    public DateTime? dateOfBirth { get; set; }
    public DateTime? lastVisitDate { get; set; }
    public string? mainContact { get; set; }
}

// Query method
public async Task<(List<PatientListDto> Items, string ContinuationToken)> GetPatientsPageAsync(
    Container indexContainer,
    string practiceId,
    string continuationToken,
    int pageSize = 20,
    string lastNamePrefix = null)
{
    var query = "SELECT c.id, c.firstName, c.lastName, c.dateOfBirth, c.lastVisitDate, c.mainContact " +
                "FROM c WHERE c.practiceId = @practiceId";

    if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(lastNamePrefix))
    {
        query += " AND STARTSWITH(c.lastName, @lastNamePrefix)";
    }
    query += " ORDER BY c.lastName ASC, c.firstName ASC"; // ensure composite index exists

    var queryDef = new QueryDefinition(query).WithParameter("@practiceId", practiceId);
    if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(lastNamePrefix))
        queryDef.WithParameter("@lastNamePrefix", lastNamePrefix);

    var requestOptions = new QueryRequestOptions
    {
        PartitionKey = new PartitionKey(practiceId),
        MaxItemCount = pageSize
    };

    var iterator = indexContainer.GetItemQueryIterator<PatientListDto>(
        queryDef,
        continuationToken,
        requestOptions);

    string newContinuation = null;
    var results = new List<PatientListDto>();

    if (iterator.HasMoreResults)
    {
        var response = await iterator.ReadNextAsync();
        newContinuation = response.ContinuationToken;
        results.AddRange(response.Resource);
    }

    return (results, newContinuation);
}
```

Client-side infinite scroll:
- Keep continuationToken from server response.
- When user scrolls near bottom, call GetPatientsPageAsync with token to get next page.
- Use small page sizes initially (20) and increase if UX requires.

5) RU estimates (practical)
- Query patient_index (pageSize=20) partitioned by practiceId:
  - Typical RU: ~1–3 RU per page (dependent on composite index and projection).
- Query patient_index (pageSize=50):
  - Typical RU: ~2–5 RU per page.
- Fetch full patient doc (point read) to open patient detail:
  - 1 RU for small patient doc without payloads — if patient doc is large (124KB) point read RU may be 2–5 RU depending on size; still cheap.
- If you instead used Option A (projection directly on patients container), expect ~3–5 RU per 20 items — similar but larger variance; Option B is more stable and cheaper.

6) Operational considerations & best practices
- Idempotency: Upserts in Change Feed are idempotent — safe to restart.
- Deletes: If you soft-delete patients (isEnabled=false), ensure Change Feed logic removes or marks index doc; if hard delete occurs, use TTL or tombstone handling (store a deleted flag in index).
- Consistency: Change Feed is eventually consistent relative to source writes. Accept small lag (usually seconds). If strict immediate reflect is required, optionally upsert synchronously in API write path for that single patient (rare need).
- Monitoring:
  - Track Change Feed errors, queue length (leases), and index upsert latency.
  - Monitor RU consumption of index container and patient_index query patterns.
- Backpressure: Implement exponential retry and dead-letter handling for failed upserts.
- Change Feed throughput: Ensure lease container has appropriate RU allocation.
- Schema evolution: Keep patient_index schema minimal; adding fields is OK (upsert handles missing fields).
- Security: patient_index contains no PHI beyond mainContact (which is PHI); ensure index container uses same practice-scoped access controls and is protected by same IAM/role-based access and encryption rules.

7) Summary recommendation (concise)
- Implement Option B: patient_index container (PK = practiceId) updated by a Change Feed worker (Azure Function).
- Use patient_index for all paginated UI list loads — page sizes 20–50, continuation tokens, composite index on [practiceId, lastName, firstName, dateOfBirth].
- Keep X12 payloads in Blob Storage (already chosen) to keep patient docs small.
- Perform an initial backfill, enable Change Feed processing, and monitor index upsert latency.
- Expected RU per page (20 items): ~1–3 RU — cost-effective.

Would you like:
- A ready-to-run Azure Function project (full files) for the Change Feed worker and backfill script?
- The exact patient_index container provisioning JSON (container throughput/indexing policy/composite index) ready to deploy?
